BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pulitzer-center-global-south-civil-society-microgrants-2026@artificialn
 ouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T205603Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pulitzer Center: Global South Civil Society Microgrants 2
 026
DESCRIPTION:The Pulitzer Center funds civil society in the Global South to 
 build civic engagement projects around Pulitzer Center-supported journalis
 m: grassroots collectives\, community-led movements\, ARTISTS and civic co
 alitions taking published reporting to the communities it concerns. This g
 rant cycle is restricted to the Center's Climate and Environment topics: R
 AINFOREST (drivers of rainforest degradation\, the role of global markets\
 , finance and policy\, centring the rights and roles of Indigenous peoples
 )\, OCEAN (degradation of marine environments\, ocean health and the clima
 te crisis\, ocean governance\, science-backed and community-led solutions)
 \, and CLIMATE AND LABOR (the intersection of climate and labour\, includi
 ng gender\, livelihoods\, adaptation and community resilience). WHAT COUNT
 S AS A PROJECT: community engagement and organising that connects reportin
 g with audiences\; creative expressions\, explicitly including digital cam
 paigns\, public performances and creative publication formats\; public dia
 logues bringing together affected communities\, journalists\, decision-mak
 ers and academia\; and other innovative uses of the reporting that build o
 n local cultural practices. Every project must use at least one published 
 Pulitzer Center-supported reporting project meaningfully\, or involve a Pu
 litzer Center-supported journalist centrally\, and must name a clear targe
 t audience. OUT OF SCOPE: journalism and reporting projects themselves (th
 ose go to the Center's reporting grants)\, anything not built on Pulitzer 
 Center-supported reporting\, direct advocacy or lobbying\, campaigns endor
 sing political candidates or parties\, and for-profit projects. WHO CAN AP
 PLY: grassroots organisations\, community educators\, civil society organi
 sations and coalitions\, youth movements and other groups working at the i
 ntersection of journalism\, civic rights and active citizenship\, with pas
 t experience running civic engagement activities in the Global South\, pro
 ven experience on the named topics\, and the operational capacity to manag
 e a microgrant from an international organisation. APPLICATION: a project 
 description of no more than 400 words\, the target audience and desired im
 pact\, your methodology\, a timeline and budget\, the lead applicant's CV 
 with a letter of recommendation\, and\, where activities involve Indigenou
 s peoples or traditional communities\, a signed or recorded statement of c
 onsent from a community member. Applications are accepted in English\, Spa
 nish\, Portuguese\, French and Indonesian through Submittable. TIMELINE: a
 pplications 15 August to 6 September 2026\, selection announced mid-Septem
 ber 2026\, onboarding October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Pulitzer Center\nLoca
 tion: Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and South
 east Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. Ci
 vil Society Microgrant instead.\nAward: USD 2\,000 to USD 4\,000 per proje
 ct. Applicants name the amount requested and submit a draft budget. Funds 
 are transferred to the partner after the agreement is signed\; partners fi
 le a financial narrative and outcomes report at the end but are NOT requir
 ed to collect detailed receipts. No application fee.\nDuration: Up to six 
 months of implementation\, October 2026 to March 2027\, with a virtual che
 ck-in in December 2026 and closure in early March 2027.\n\nMore: https://p
 ulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/global-south-
 civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://pulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/g
 lobal-south-civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and Sou
 theast Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. 
 Civil Society Microgrant instead.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:africa-no-filter-voices-for-climate-justice-2026@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T205603Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: Africa No Filter: Voices for Climate Justice 2026
DESCRIPTION:Voices for Climate Justice supports African storytellers making
  work about how climate finance reaches the continent as debt rather than 
 as grants. The framing of the call: African nations are being pushed furth
 er into debt through loans despite the continent's minimal contribution to
  the crisis\, with many governments spending more on debt repayment than o
 n health and climate adaptation combined. WHO IT IS FOR: journalists\, con
 tent creators\, podcasters\, vloggers\, muralists and data visualizers. WH
 AT YOU GET: a 12-month programme delivered with Comic Relief giving 15 to 
 20 storytellers training in climate justice\, data storytelling and narrat
 ive framing\, then mentorship and work with researchers and partners to de
 velop accurate\, research-based stories\, plus content amplification and g
 rants of up to USD 4\,000. ELIGIBILITY: aged 18 or over\; based in one of 
 the ten listed countries\; an active and highly engaged platform (podcast\
 , YouTube channel\, TikTok\, Facebook\, Instagram\, publication or data vi
 sualization portfolio) running for more than a year\; a combined audience 
 of 5\,000 or more followers across Instagram\, TikTok\, Facebook\, Twitter
 /X and Snapchat for content creators and data visualizers\, while journali
 sts may instead demonstrate reach through publication circulation\; conten
 t creators must be unsigned and not represented by an agent\, though journ
 alists may be affiliated with a publication provided the submitted work an
 d content rights remain their own\; a commitment to publishing at least fo
 ur pieces on gender\, debt and fossil fuels over at least six months\; and
  individual applicants only\, no organisations or agencies. Proposals must
  focus on the campaign's core themes of climate justice\, gender\, debt an
 d fossil fuels in Africa. NOT CONSIDERED: incomplete applications\, postal
  or email submissions\, hacked material\, vulgar\, racist\, sexist or homo
 phobic material\, work reinforcing stereotypes of Africa as poor\, corrupt
 \, diseased\, conflict-ridden or lacking agency\, and accounts or posts bo
 osted by paid engagement. HOW TO APPLY: prepare a pitch deck of no more th
 an 5 slides or a video of no more than 60 seconds connecting gender\, debt
  and fossil fuels in Africa\, then submit through the ANF application port
 al with links to your platforms and a short description of the audience yo
 u plan to reach. Content may be delivered in English or French. Selection 
 weighs originality of the idea\, relevance to ANF's mission of storytellin
 g beyond stereotypes\, relevance to the gender-debt-fossil fuel focus\, an
 d reach or standing on your platform or in your community. Only shortliste
 d candidates are notified.\n\nOrganisation: Africa No Filter\, in partners
 hip with Comic Relief\nLocation: Applicants must be based in Mozambique\, 
 Uganda\, Nigeria\, Angola\, Chad\, the Republic of Congo\, Tanzania\, Sout
 h Sudan\, Malawi or Zambia.\nAward: Grants of up to USD 4\,000 (the call s
 tates a range of USD 0 to 4\,000)\, alongside training\, mentorship and co
 ntent amplification. Between 15 and 20 storytellers will be supported. No 
 application fee.\nDuration: 12-month programme. Applications opened 11 Aug
 ust 2026 and are reviewed on a rolling basis\; deadline 25 September 2026 
 at 18:00 GMT. Screening 5 October\, interviews 9 October\, outcomes commun
 icated in the first week of October and grantees announced 15 October 2026
 .\n\nMore: https://africanofilter.org/what-we-do/community/voices-for-clim
 ate-justice/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://africanofilter.org/what-we-do/community/voices-for-climate-just
 ice/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicants must be based in Mozambique\, Uganda\, Nigeria\, Angola
 \, Chad\, the Republic of Congo\, Tanzania\, South Sudan\, Malawi or Zambi
 a.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:africa-no-filter-kekere-storytellers-fund-2026@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260901T205603Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261231
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270101
SUMMARY:Deadline: Africa No Filter: Kekere Storytellers Fund 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Kekere Storytellers Fund pays micro-grants to emerging Afri
 can storytellers who are already creating and publishing work that offers 
 fresh and alternative perspectives on the continent\, showing an Africa th
 at is innovative\, evolving and creative rather than defined by poverty\, 
 conflict\, disease\, poor leadership and corruption. Africa No Filter desc
 ribes the fund as its way of getting funding\, which is limited and rarely
  accessed by up-and-coming storytellers\, to artists\, journalists and con
 tent creators. PREREQUISITE: applicants must first be members of Africa No
  Filter's African Narrative Collective\, so join that before applying. ELI
 GIBILITY: African\, based anywhere in Africa or the diaspora\; under 35\; 
 able to work in English or French\; a track record of ground-breaking work
  that challenges stereotypical narratives about your community\, country o
 r the continent\; at least two years of related work experience\; a minimu
 m of 5\,000 followers across Twitter\, Facebook\, TikTok\, Instagram and Y
 ouTube\; and falling into at least one of two categories\, MEDIA AND CONTE
 NT CREATORS (journalist\, blogger\, vlogger\, podcaster\, fiction and non-
 fiction writer\, photographer) or ARTISTS (poet\, musician\, spoken word p
 oet\, dancer\, painter\, sculptor\, illustrator\, animator). NOT ELIGIBLE:
  filmmakers\, who are directed to ANF's Last Mile Film Fund instead\; stor
 ytellers without evidence of narrative-shifting work\; requests to buy equ
 ipment such as cameras or laptops\; and requests to host or attend events 
 such as workshops\, festivals or conferences. WHAT ANF IS LOOKING FOR: an 
 interesting\, unique story about Africa that amplifies unheard voices\, ch
 allenges global perspectives and introduces contemporary narratives\, with
  a clear sense of what you want the reader or viewer to take away and the 
 impact you want to create locally and globally.\n\nOrganisation: Africa No
  Filter\nLocation: Africans based anywhere in Africa or the diaspora.\nAwa
 rd: Micro-grants of USD 500 to USD 3\,000. Between 12 and 14 storytellers 
 will be selected. No application fee.\nDuration: Rolling. Applications sta
 y open until the end of the year or until 12 to 14 storytellers have been 
 selected\, whichever comes first\, so apply early rather than close to the
  nominal end date.\n\nMore: https://africanofilter.org/what-we-do/communit
 y/kekere-storytellers-fund/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://africanofilter.org/what-we-do/community/kekere-storytellers-fun
 d/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Africans based anywhere in Africa or the diaspora.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
